Date: Fri, 29 Jan 93 16:43:41 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Zero-Sum Games

O.K., I've read up a bit on game theory and want to modify my previous
position.  A zero-sum game is defined as one where there is some way
to rescale and rezero each player's utilities, such that for every
possible combination of strategies that the different players could
choose, the expected utilities of the players after the game sum to
zero.  

Thus a constant and uniform rise in everyone's utilities is
irrelevant; this concept offers no related concept of positive vs.
negative sum game.  Note also that this is defined in terms of
strategies, not states of the world.  Therefore the fact that someone
might end up better without others being worse is irrelevant, if not
predictable ahead of time.

So are real financial markets, such as equities, futures, betting,
etc., zero-sum?  It seems clear to me now that they are not zero sum
exactly to the extent that they influence behaviour *outside* those
markets.  Stock markets are zero-sum (ignoring transaction costs) as
long as everyone is just trading stock - but when people introduce new
companies into the market, or buy old ones out of the market, then the
markets start to influence the actual allocation of resources in the
world.  Similarly, when corporations change their behaviour based on
their stock price, that is also non-zero sum trades between employees
and corporations.

But a similar fraction of lots of zero-sum trades to a few non-zero
sum induced actions (or insurance hedges) is also true of commodity
futures, options, etc.  One might argue there are fewer induced useful
actions by football betting markets, but otherwise I see little reason
to distinguish futures from stocks from ... on this criteria.

Robin Hanson

