Date: Fri, 29 Jan 93 18:37:15 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Zero-Sum Games

From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> O.K., I've read up a bit on game theory and want to modify my previous
> position.  A zero-sum game is defined as one where there is some way
> to rescale and rezero each player's utilities, such that for every
> possible combination of strategies that the different players could
> choose, the expected utilities of the players after the game sum to
> zero.  

This is a different definition of zero-sum than what I have heard before,
but I can see the logic of it.  Let's see if I've got it straight.
Consider a classic matching game - players say either A or B, and if
they match, the first player wins 1 from the other, and if they are
different, the second player wins 1 from the first.  Game theory would
graph this as:
                     2nd
                    Player
                 A         B
             -------------------
 1st       A | (1,-1) | (-1,1) |
             -------------------
 Player    B | (-1,1) | (1,-1) |
             -------------------

This is obviously a zero-sum game (assuming utilities == dollars).  Now,
Robin's point is that we could double each number and add 2, getting:

                     2nd
                    Player
                 A         B
             -----------------
 1st       A | (4,0) | (0,4) |
             -----------------
 Player    B | (0,4) | (4,0) |
             -----------------

And even though this looks superficially like a non-zero-sum game, it is
actually equivalent, in the strategic sense, to playing the first game
for double stakes and being paid 2 independent of the game.  So the
"underlying" game is still zero sum.

And, as Robin says, this only allows you to describe games as zero-sum
or not.  "Positive sum" and "negative sum" are an incomplete
classification set anyway, since in general games may have some net
positive and some net negative outcomes.

> So are real financial markets, such as equities, futures, betting,
> etc., zero-sum?  It seems clear to me now that they are not zero sum 
> exactly to the extent that they influence behaviour *outside* those 
> markets.  Stock markets are zero-sum (ignoring transaction costs) as 
> long as everyone is just trading stock - but when people introduce new 
> companies into the market, or buy old ones out of the market, then the 
> markets start to influence the actual allocation of resources in the 
> world.  Similarly, when corporations change their behaviour based on 
> their stock price, that is also non-zero sum trades between employees 
> and corporations.  

I think this overlooks the payment of dividends.  Different stocks pay
at different rates, and my strategies include the option of staying
out of the market entirely, so I would not suppose that they can be
scaled out of the equation.  Dividends, at least in some theories, are
what actually give stocks their value, and some argue that the predicted
future dividend pattern can actually be used to predict "correct" stock
prices.  So they are a very important part of the market.

Dividends aren't paid for futures, so this seems like a difference.

It's also not clear to me how useful it is to assume that utility is equal
to money value.  It seems to me that trades might not occur at all if
this were literally true.  In a trade, I give up commodity A and get B,
while my partner does the opposite.  Evidentally this represents an increase
in utility for both of us, else we wouldn't trade.  Graphing it, we see:

                     2nd
                    Player
               Trade    Don't
             -----------------
 1st   Trade | (+,+) | (0,0) |
 Player      -----------------
       Don't | (0,0) | (0,0) |
             -----------------

Here I am assuming a trade will take place only if both want to; if neither
one wants to, the status quo remains, which I show as 0.  This looks non-
zero-sum by Robin's definition.  So it's questionable whether even simple
trades are zero-sum.

Hal

