Date:     Sat, 30 Jan 93 9:50:55 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: State Evolution

Garret Goebel and Hal have hit upon an interesting question: Is socialism
going away?  Many seem to think that the demise of the USSR heralds this.
However, many generals of the Red Army see the present situation as an
aberration.  There are other reasons libertarians should be interested in
the evolution of statism going on - and even any extropians who aren't
libertarians!

According to Paul Johnson's interpretation of recent history in his book
Modern Times, the reason why the USSR dissolved is because the leader(s)
of the CCCP opposed use of the military to crush domestic insurgency, as
was done by the Butchers of Beijing in Tiananmen Square just a short while
earlier.

If this is correct, then if opportinity to use the military to re-establish
Empire of some sort arises and is taken, then in many ways we'll be back to
Square 1.  There will still be an Evil Empire in the East.

But will it be socialist?  This begs the question of what socialism is,
anyways.  The classical definition hasn't withstood the test of time very
well.  Few who call themselves or other socialists do so in the original
sense of the term.

I look at the question in terms of the three basic aspects of property
rights: the rights to possession, use, and disposal of property.  As
originally defined, socialism consisted in the taking of these three aspects
of property rights outright to be held and managed in trust by some sort
of central planning agency.

However, in theory and practice this soon proved to be impossible when put
to the test.  Ludwig von Mises and Friedrich Hayek held socialism at bay
in theory during the 1920s and 1930s, and even Lenin had to implement his
New Economic Policy soon after seizing power in Moscow in the 1920s.
Thereafter, socialists in theory and practice let go of taking away the
right to possess property completely, and settled for taking some of it
and much of the rest of the three.

What, then did this innovation make of Socialism?  Fascism, in Italy,
Germany, and elsewhere.

Somehow, socialism came to mean redistributionism in the USA.  While not
absent in practice, redistrbutionism seems to be largely dead in theory
in the USA.  Perhaps our contributors from outside the USA can fill us in
on the state of affairs there.  Both Mondale and Dukakis ran as redistribu-
ists and lost.  Reagan, Bush, and now Clinton ran as supporters of economic
growth and won.  Cynics like me will say that this is because they needed
the economy to grow in order to keep financing their redistributionism,
but this theory is no longer championed in US presidential politics.

If the redistributionist- or welfare-state is dead in theory, then it won't
be long for this world in practice, either.  Does this then mean that the
State will wither away as Marx predicted?  That might be nice, but all
the signs are that the State will merely evolve into a new form, and adapt
itself to the changes that have taken place.

I think that we are witnessing the metamorphosis of the redistributionist
State into the Therepeutic State, as Thomas Szasz, the libertarian
psychiatrist, has dubbed it.  Social engineering having gone out of fashion,
Social Therapy is taking its place.  Central planners are being replaced
with Public Health agencies such as the FDA, the EPA, etc.  The Command
Economy is being replaced by Command Medicine.

The evidence for this has been building for quite some time now, but until
the fall of the Redistributionist State, the Therapeutic State has been
waiting in the wings as understudy.  The former can't go on, and the latter
isn't about to let the opportunity presented go by without making an effort.

The evidence which persuades me the most is that old "welfare-state" policies
are being remodeled with a Therapeutic State veneer: drug prohibition is
being rejustified on the grounds that illegal drugs are toxic poisonous
pollutants; gun controls are being rejustifed on the grounds of Public Health
and Safety; automobile users are being forced to use seat belts and other
safety devices to reduce their risk of getting injured in an accident;
motorcyclists are being forced to wear helmets for the same reason; our
new US President appears eager to force us all to have medical insurance
one way or another, and at taxpayer expense.

Old State agencies are grasping to seize more power: the FDA's activities
are familiar to most of us; the EPA's likely to do similarly under the
administration of Vice President Gore, who seems to think it impossible
for environmental regulation to harm the economic growth his running-mate
gives so much lip-service to.

That appears to exhaust my brainstorm for now, but please elaborate upon
this theme and its variations.

