 KMOSTA01@ulkyvx02.louisville.edu (KMOSTA01@ulkyvx02.louisville.edu) writes:
>
>The Soviet Union had a high growth rate in the 1930's?
>Very interesting. Is that why the Soviet soldiers who invaded Poland
>in 1939 did not know what a handwatch was and were impressed to see
>sausage?

 Yes, I'm theorizing that by redirecting production from goods
that the average person values to things that the government values,
such as factories, they were able to increase production at a faster
rate than they otherwise would. I should note that what was increased
was the value of production to the government; the average person
was prevented from valuing most goods (in the economic sense of expressing
a value).

>Is that why eight to ten million Ukrainians were killed or starved
>and millions were sent to Siberian camps?

 No, the only connection I see between the genocide and the economic
policies was that they both require a powerfull government which is
indifferent to the well-being of its subjects.

>But then again, they went off the double-entry accounting in the 1920's
>and came back to it in 1930's so any arrangement was possible.
>To make such a claim about the Soviet Union is an utter nonsense.

 Not nonsense, speculation. Some of the accountants were shot for
reporting unpopular statistics, so we know that the actual growth
rate was less than reported, but there is no reliable way to know
the quantitative effects of the fraud.
 Enough of this unextropian subject. Nobody is defending the belief
that economic growth should be maximized at any cost, and I think
the person who accused Libertarians of holding that belief can see
that he was exaggerating.
